[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186335310 :answer-field/choices 17592186334688] [:db/retract 17592186335310 :answer-field/choices 17592186335106] [:db/retract 17592186335310 :answer-field/choices 17592186333061] [:db/retract 17592186335310 :answer-field/choices 17592186335029] [:db/retract 17592186335310 :answer-field/choices 17592186333852] [:db/retract 17592186335645 :answer-field/choices 17592186334486] [:db/retract 17592186335645 :answer-field/choices 17592186334055] [:db/retract 17592186335645 :answer-field/choices 17592186333660] [:db/retract 17592186335645 :answer-field/choices 17592186333612] [:db/retract 17592186335645 :answer-field/choices 17592186333773] {:db/id 17592186172596 :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})=[]+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335310 :answer-field/correct "answer-80b40976-92df-5d2c-86ee-4dc5ee7ca40b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a7a61d3-30ae-5e41-aea0-85e5dcd146d4" "answer-78ddad5a-92bc-5076-b3a4-c6e43df1cb7e" "answer-80b40976-92df-5d2c-86ee-4dc5ee7ca40b" "answer-805f4e52-0148-5922-8396-e0236216c0d8" "answer-e61bdf0f-ed23-51fc-a2c4-a20c5b93a968"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a7a61d3-30ae-5e41-aea0-85e5dcd146d4" :answer/id #uuid "9a7a61d3-30ae-5e41-aea0-85e5dcd146d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78ddad5a-92bc-5076-b3a4-c6e43df1cb7e" :answer/id #uuid "78ddad5a-92bc-5076-b3a4-c6e43df1cb7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80b40976-92df-5d2c-86ee-4dc5ee7ca40b" :answer/id #uuid "80b40976-92df-5d2c-86ee-4dc5ee7ca40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-805f4e52-0148-5922-8396-e0236216c0d8" :answer/id #uuid "805f4e52-0148-5922-8396-e0236216c0d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e61bdf0f-ed23-51fc-a2c4-a20c5b93a968" :answer/id #uuid "e61bdf0f-ed23-51fc-a2c4-a20c5b93a968" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335995 :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})+[]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When 